<section class="bg-base-100 text-base-content">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[80rem] px-5 pt-14 md:px-8 md:pt-20">
    <div data-reveal>
      <p class="typed text-base-content/75">Since 2018 · Marquette, Madison</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-[16ch] text-[clamp(2.75rem,5vw+1rem,5rem)] leading-[1]">Our story</h1>
    </div>
    <img src="/media/old-counter.jpg" class="mt-10 aspect-[4/3] w-full rounded-box object-cover sm:aspect-[21/9]">
  </div>
  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-[80rem] gap-12 px-5 py-20 md:grid-cols-3 md:gap-10 md:px-8 md:py-24" data-reveal="stagger">
    <article>
      <p class="typed text-base-content/70">01</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-[1.75rem] leading-[1.1]">The pharmacy and its window</h2>
      <p class="body-copy mt-4">For seventy years the corner of Larkspur Row was Haugen&rsquo;s Pharmacy, with its oak counter downstairs and the pharmacist&rsquo;s flat behind the bay window upstairs, the oriel. When it closed, the counter stayed. We sanded it, kept the drawers, and named the café after the window.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="typed text-base-content/70">02</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-[1.75rem] leading-[1.1]">A cook and a barista</h2>
      <p class="body-copy mt-4">Tessa grew up on a dairy farm near Mount Horeb and cooked breakfast in Chicago for ten years. Amir ran the coffee bar of a Milwaukee roaster. They met at a friend&rsquo;s brunch, argued about hash browns, and opened Oriel in the spring of 2018.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="typed text-base-content/70">03</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 text-[1.75rem] leading-[1.1]">Bread at five</h2>
      <p class="body-copy mt-4">June comes in at five to bake the day&rsquo;s bread, buns and scones, so the toast is from our own oven and the bakery case is full when the door opens at seven. What is left at three goes to the shelter on Williamson Street.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
